// @check
// @error-at 12:20 i32 doesn't fit in EntityId — the conversion is lossy and must be made explicit with `as` (write `x as EntityId`)
// A base-typed VALUE does not flow into a newtype — the direction that makes the
// type worth having, since every id in an engine starts life as some `i32`.
//
// The INVERTED CONTROL is this same file with `new` deleted from the declaration:
// it checks clean, which is what makes this reject attributable to the newtype and
// not to anything else in the program. The literal spelling `const e: EntityId = 1`
// is accepted (`newtype-scalar-distinctness.vl`) — a literal has no prior identity
// to confuse — so it is a VARIABLE, deliberately, that is used here.
type EntityId = new i32
const raw = 7
const e: EntityId = raw
print(e)
